    "body": "Fixes #42508\n\n## Problem\n\nDeleting a terminal buffer while a write to its PTY is still pending hangs Nvim indefinitely. `chansend()` immediately followed by `nvim_buf_delete()` reproduces it:\n\n```lua\nlocal job = vim.fn.jobstart({\n  '/bin/sh', '-c', 'printf \"ready\\\\n\"; exec cat',\n}, { term = true })\n\nvim.wait(1000, function()\n  return vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(0, 0, 1, false)[1] == 'ready'\nend)\n\n-- Write and delete before libuv completes the write.\nvim.fn.chansend(job, 'pending write\\n')\nvim.api.nvim_buf_delete(0, { force = true })\n\n-- Process queued events immediately after deletion.\nvim.wait(1, function() return false end)\n```\n\n```\ntimeout -k 1s 3s nvim --headless -u NONE -i NONE --noplugin -n -l repro.lua\n```\n\nNo output is printed and Nvim hangs until it is killed. Interrupting the build in a debugger shows `term_delayed_free` spinning in `multiqueue_put_event` from `multiqueue_process_events`, reached from `nlua_loop_poll` (i.e. from `vim.wait()`'s event processing).\n\n## Root cause\n\n- `chan->events` is a child queue of `main_loop.events` (`multiqueue_new_child()` in `channel_terminal_open`).\n- When a terminal buffer is deleted, `term_close()` queues `term_delayed_free()` on `chan->events`.\n- `term_delayed_free()` must wait until both streams have no `pending_reqs` (in-flight write/read requests), and currently it *retries* by re-queueing itself onto `chan->events`.\n- `multiqueue_process_events()` drains a queue `while (!multiqueue_empty(self))`: an event that re-queues itself onto the queue being drained keeps that queue permanently non-empty, so the drain loop never returns.\n- Because the drain loop never returns, libuv never turns, so the pending write request's callback never runs, so `pending_reqs` never clears — a livelock.\n\nThis is the same self-rescheduling pattern fixed for `emit_termrequest()` by #32755 (via `term->pending.events`): that one livelocked `vim.wait()` in #32753, this one reaches the separate cleanup path in `channel.c`.\n\n## Solution\n\nReschedule the retry through the main loop instead of re-queueing onto the queue being drained:\n\n```diff\n   if (chan->stream.proc.in.pending_reqs || chan->stream.proc.out.s.pending_reqs) {\n-    multiqueue_put(chan->events, term_delayed_free, chan);\n+    // Reschedule via the main loop: re-queueing onto chan->events would livelock\n+    // multiqueue_process_events() (it drains a queue until empty), and starving libuv\n+    // prevents the pending write request from ever completing. #42508\n+    loop_schedule_deferred(&main_loop, event_create(term_delayed_free, chan));\n     return;\n   }\n```\n\n`loop_schedule_deferred()` routes the retry through `thread_events` → `async_cb` → `fast_events` → `loop_deferred_event` → `main_loop.events`, so libuv gets a turn between retries and the pending write request can complete. A plain `multiqueue_put(main_loop.events, …)` would **not** be enough, because in the hang scenario the retry is processed from inside an ongoing `multiqueue_process_events(main_loop.events)` call — the detour through `thread_events`/`fast_events` is what guarantees a libuv turn between retries.\n\nEverything else is unchanged: the retry still only frees the terminal once both streams are idle, and the channel itself continues to be freed on a later pass (`channel_decref()` → `free_channel_event`), so there is no change to the shutdown or reference-counting behavior. `on_channel_data()` already guards on `chan->term`, so processing a queued read event after the terminal is destroyed remains safe.\n\n## Testing\n\nNew functional test in `test/functional/terminal/buffer_spec.lua` (next to the existing \"external interrupt (got_int) does not hang #20726\" test) mirrors the repro:\n\n- starts a long-running PTY job with `jobstart(…, {term=true})` and waits for `tty ready`,\n- calls `chansend()` and `nvim_buf_delete()` in the same event loop iteration, before libuv completes the write,\n- drains the queued events with `vim.wait()`, then asserts the buffer is gone.\n\nVerified locally (Linux, `0.13.0-dev-187d13a`):\n\n- **Patched build**: the test passes (~60 ms), repeatedly.\n- **Unpatched build** (only the `channel.c` change reverted, test kept): the test run stalls and is killed by the runner timeout — confirming the test reproduces the hang from #42508. Like the #20726 test, a regression here manifests as a stalled session, which the test runner's `TEST_TIMEOUT` catches.\n- Full `test/functional/terminal` group re-run on the patched build: all green (buffer/altscreen/api/highlight/tui/window specs, 0 failures).\n",
